  "words": [
   {
    "word": "metamorphoō (μεταμορφόω)",
    "lang": "Greek",
    "claimed": "‘Transfigure’ into a ‘higher form’. The preacher calls ‘transformed’ in Rom 12:2 a bad translation and says the word means turning humanity into divinity, the same as Jesus’ transfiguration.",
    "lexicon": "BDAG: to change into another form, transform. Used of Jesus’ transfiguration (Matt 17:2; Mark 9:2) and of moral or spiritual change in believers (Rom 12:2; 2 Cor 3:18). The meta- prefix marks change, not a ‘higher’ form. ‘Transformed’ and ‘transfigured’ both translate it correctly. The link between the passages is real, but ‘turning humanity into divinity’ is an interpretation, not part of the word’s meaning.",
    "verdict": "partly"
   },
   {
    "word": "doxa (δόξα)",
    "lang": "Greek",
    "claimed": "‘The thoughts and opinions of’. It has no character of its own and takes on the character of what it refers to, so Moses’ face shone with the law’s ‘opinion’ of disapproval and death.",
    "lexicon": "In classical Greek doxa does mean opinion, expectation or reputation. In the LXX and the NT it mostly translates Hebrew kabod and means glory, splendour, brightness or honour (BDAG 1–3). In 2 Cor 3:7 it refers to visible radiance. The ‘opinion’ sense does not fit this passage, and the text does not say the radiance was ‘disapproval’.",
    "verdict": "not supported"
   },
   {
    "word": "shamayim (שָׁמַיִם), ‘heavens’",
    "lang": "Hebrew",
    "claimed": "The plural in Gen 1:1 shows heaven has levels (first, second, third heaven).",
    "lexicon": "Shamayim always has a plural or dual-looking form in Hebrew, even when it means just the sky, so the form alone does not prove levels. Some Jewish and early Christian writers did teach several heavens (compare 2 Cor 12:2), but the preacher’s specific map (seen and created, unseen and created, heaven in God) is his own theological model.",
    "verdict": "partly"
   },
   {
    "word": "‘mind of Christ’ (nous Christou)",
    "lang": "Greek",
    "claimed": "A possessive genitive: believers have Christ’s own mind, not a mind He made and gave them.",
    "lexicon": "Grammatically it is a possessive or subjective genitive (‘Christ’s mind’). In context (1 Cor 2:12–16) commentators usually take it to mean the Spirit-given understanding of Christ’s ways, not literally sharing Christ’s divine mind. The grammar supports ‘Christ’s mind’, but how far to push that is a matter of interpretation.",
    "verdict": "partly"
   }
  ],
