  "questions": [
   {
    "q": "Why does the series say approaching church as a provider of needs leads to disappointment?",
    "a": "Because the church is not for meeting needs but for producing mature sons. It is meant to confront, challenge, change and hold you accountable, and families gather around covenant rather than need."
   },
   {
    "q": "How does this part define the glory of God?",
    "a": "Not as clouds or felt presence but as God’s intention made manifest: Jesus become flesh, creation and humanity itself. So the earth full of glory means people knowing who they are."
   },
   {
    "q": "What was the Roman strategy the preacher sees behind ekklēsia and apostles?",
    "a": "Instead of plundering conquered lands, Rome sent envoys to set up a governing assembly that replaced the local system and made the colony like the empire. Jesus used this picture: the church turns its region into heaven."
   },
   {
    "q": "What does it mean to discern the body?",
    "a": "To see one another as God sees us, regarding no one according to the flesh and recognising what each carries. Failure to do this, with judgment and bitterness, is linked to weakness, sickness and death."
   },
   {
    "q": "What does the Nazareth story teach about familiarity?",
    "a": "The people moved from astonished to offended when they recalled Jesus’ family, seeing Him as a mere man, and so He could do few miracles there. Familiarity plus immaturity breeds contempt and blocks impartation."
   },
   {
    "q": "Why should Christians not try to create unity?",
    "a": "Because unity already exists in heaven’s Zion; attempts to manufacture it reduce everything to the lowest common denominator. Believers are told to preserve it by looking past doctrine and preference and gathering as family."
   },
   {
    "q": "What is the difference between power and authority here?",
    "a": "Power comes from God, while authority is conferred within the body and holds the local church together. Jesus was empowered by the Father but authorised by His cousin John."
   },
   {
    "q": "Why is John the Revelator held up as a model?",
    "a": "He saw both the risen Christ and the resurrected bride, discerned the body, and wrote that as He is so are we; the preacher links this to his not dying."
   }
  ],
